{
  "preference": "A",
  "reason": "A better supports the requested decision: it fits the 180–260-word brief, explicitly calculates the zero-percentage-point difference-in-differences, and proposes a decision rule for a randomized offer. B exceeds the word limit substantially and introduces an insufficiently qualified suggestion to estimate effects among reminder users. B more clearly addresses missing earlier trends, but its claim that a test without a preset threshold would be 'as uninformative as the first' overstates the limitation.",
  "reports": {
    "A": {
      "task_fidelity": {
        "score": 4,
        "reason": "Provides the requested concise director note, comparisons, design assessment, feasible next test, decision criterion, and source-ID citations within the requested word range.",
        "excerpt": "Descriptive results: 'The difference-in-differences estimate is therefore 0 percentage points'; Proposed next test: 'Randomize an offer of reminders next month'.",
        "source_evidence": "S1 supplies the four counts and group sizes; S2 states that staff can randomize an offer and management must set the smallest worthwhile benefit before examining results."
      },
      "facts_and_evidence": {
        "score": 4,
        "reason": "All numerical comparisons are correct and source-grounded. The suggested three-point threshold is clearly an example, not an established management preference.",
        "excerpt": "Descriptive results: '+5 percentage points, or +6.7% relative' and '+5 points, or +9.1% relative'; Proposed next test: 'for example, 3 percentage points'.",
        "source_evidence": "S1: prior counts were 75 and 55, pilot counts 80 and 60, with 100 borrowers in each group. S2: 'There is no preset estimate of the smallest worthwhile benefit'."
      },
      "explanation_and_synthesis": {
        "score": 4,
        "reason": "Clearly connects the unchanged baseline gap and equal improvements to the failure of the raw pilot comparison to demonstrate causality, then proposes randomization to improve identification.",
        "excerpt": "Descriptive results: 'The raw between-group gap remained 20 points ... but that gap already existed before the pilot'; Design assessment: 'the 5-point within-group gains cannot be attributed to reminders'.",
        "source_evidence": "S1 establishes identical five-point gains and an unchanged twenty-point gap. S2 establishes voluntary enrollment and a concurrent amnesty reaching both groups."
      },
      "counterevidence_and_boundaries": {
        "score": 3,
        "reason": "Recognizes selection and the concurrent intervention, but omits the missing earlier trends and the resulting inability to assess parallel trends. 'No reminder effect is detectable' is less precise than saying the causal effect is not identified.",
        "excerpt": "Design assessment: 'The appropriate causal claim is that no reminder effect is detectable from this design'.",
        "source_evidence": "S2: 'Earlier trends and reasons for opting in were not recorded.' A zero descriptive difference-in-differences does not establish a zero causal effect without additional assumptions."
      },
      "reader_usefulness": {
        "score": 4,
        "reason": "Gives a clear immediate conclusion and an actionable randomized-offer test with required records, an intention-to-treat decision target, and a prospectively chosen benefit threshold.",
        "excerpt": "Proposed next test: 'If the intention-to-treat effect is at least that threshold and statistically distinguishable from zero, expand the reminder offer; otherwise, do not scale it.'",
        "source_evidence": "S2 explicitly permits randomizing the offer and recording assignment, uptake, and return status, and requires management to choose a worthwhile-benefit threshold before examining results."
      }
    },
    "B": {
      "task_fidelity": {
        "score": 2,
        "reason": "Addresses the substantive components and cites both sources, but substantially exceeds the explicit 180–260-word limit. It describes the zero difference-in-differences without stating that calculated quantity explicitly.",
        "excerpt": "What the numbers show: 'Both groups improved by exactly 5 points. The group difference therefore did not widen or narrow'; closing note: 'No independent review was conducted; the assessment is the author's own and is provisional.'",
        "source_evidence": "The brief requires a 180–260-word note. S1 supports the comparisons; S2 supports the proposed randomized offer. The closing disclaimer is unnecessary to meet those requirements."
      },
      "facts_and_evidence": {
        "score": 3,
        "reason": "Reports the supplied counts and implementation facts accurately, but the user-effect proposal needs identification assumptions not supplied by the sources. The claim that omitting a threshold makes a randomized test equally uninformative is unsupported.",
        "excerpt": "Recommended next test: 'separately examine uptake to estimate the effect among those who used reminders'; Decision criterion: 'Without this preset, the second test will be as uninformative as the first.'",
        "source_evidence": "S2 permits randomization of an offer and recording uptake; it does not make uptake randomized. Its requirement for a preset management threshold does not imply that random assignment without that threshold yields no useful causal evidence."
      },
      "explanation_and_synthesis": {
        "score": 3,
        "reason": "Strongly explains the baseline imbalance, equal changes, and missing trends, but weakens the synthesis by conflating causal information from randomization with decision usefulness from a preset benefit threshold.",
        "excerpt": "Why the design cannot support a causal claim: 'Earlier trends and reasons for opting in were not recorded'; Decision criterion: 'Without this preset, the second test will be as uninformative as the first.'",
        "source_evidence": "S2 separately identifies nonrandom enrollment, absent earlier trends, the feasibility of randomized offers, and the need for a management threshold. These address different evidential and decision problems."
      },
      "counterevidence_and_boundaries": {
        "score": 3,
        "reason": "Explicitly covers missing trends, selection, and amnesty, but does not bound the suggested effect estimate among users: conditioning on uptake can reintroduce selection, and a causal user-effect estimate requires additional assumptions.",
        "excerpt": "Why the design cannot support a causal claim: 'neither pre-existing trajectories nor selection motives can be ruled out'; Recommended next test: 'estimate the effect among those who used reminders'.",
        "source_evidence": "S2 states that enrollment was voluntary and only the next offer can be randomized. Neither source provides assumptions sufficient to identify a causal effect among actual users."
      },
      "reader_usefulness": {
        "score": 3,
        "reason": "Provides a useful intention-to-treat comparison and prospective adoption criterion, but unnecessary length and the unqualified uptake analysis could distract or mislead implementation.",
        "excerpt": "Recommended next test: 'Compare on-time return rates between assignment groups (intention-to-treat)'; Decision criterion: 'Adopt reminders only if the estimated effect reaches that threshold and the confidence interval excludes no benefit.'",
        "source_evidence": "S2 supports recording assignment, uptake, and return status and prospectively setting a worthwhile improvement. Assignment-group comparison directly uses the proposed randomization; a user-only causal comparison does not automatically do so."
      }
    }
  },
  "condition_clues": "After judgment: B includes a provenance-style disclaimer ('No independent review was conducted') and a separate source footer; A uses compact bold section labels and inline citations. These are possible stylistic clues only, not reliable evidence of authorship, tools, or condition, and were not rewarded."
}
